From 094d5e6920a08deda4eb79519e70ed4b970abf92 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Raza <raza@bukc.edu.pk>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 09:06:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(nav+auth): remove standalone My Borrows access; detect
 cross-tab session replacement
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two fixes.

1. MY BORROWS NO LONGER A STANDALONE UI DESTINATION. Now that every
   equipment card on Availability links to its own product-page-style
   request screen (?type=<id>), the generic My Borrows nav button (Student
   dashboard) and the Profile 'Request Equipment ->' link (which pointed at
   the bare /my-borrows) were redundant with — and a visibly worse version
   of — the same underlying screen. Removed both nav references. The route,
   the screen itself, and its compact-picker fallback all stay fully
   functional and untouched — the per-equipment flow depends on this exact
   mechanism (?type= lands on the same MyBorrowsScreen). Profile's 'Request
   Equipment ->' now points at /availability instead.

2. CROSS-TAB SESSION REPLACEMENT (the 'I see the other tab's dashboard'
   bug). Root cause: the refresh token lives in one HTTP-only cookie
   scoped to the browser origin, not per-tab — this is fundamental to how
   cookies work, not an application bug. If a Super Admin is signed in on
   one tab and a Coordinator signs in on another tab of the SAME browser,
   the second login overwrites the one shared cookie. The first tab
   doesn't notice right away (its access token is still valid in memory),
   but the next time that token expires and api.ts transparently refreshes
   on its behalf, the cookie now belongs to the OTHER account — so the
   silent refresh would previously come back as the wrong identity with
   zero signal to the user, who'd just start seeing someone else's
   dashboard mid-session.

   This can't be 'fixed' into two simultaneous different logins coexisting
   in one browser's tabs (that needs separate cookie jars — separate
   browser profiles, an Incognito window, or a different browser) — but
   the SILENT, UNSIGNALED part is exactly what's fixable and dangerous.
   api.ts now tracks which identity each tab itself believes it's
   authenticated as; the transparent-refresh path compares the server's
   response against that before accepting it, and on a mismatch clears the
   token, fires a 'session replaced' event, and refuses to adopt the new
   identity. auth.tsx listens for that event, clears local user state, and
   shows a persistent, clearly-worded banner explaining what happened and
   that the person needs to sign in again — rather than silently swapping
   the rendered dashboard underneath them. A fresh page load in a tab that
   never had an established identity is unaffected (nothing to compare
   against yet, same as opening a brand new tab) — only an
   already-active session's silent background refresh is guarded.

Verified: tsc --noEmit clean, full production build clean. No server
changes in this patch (both fixes are client-only).
